<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Architecture', href: '/docs/architecture' }, { label: 'Agents', href: '/docs/architecture/agents' }, { label: 'SENTINEL (Reviewer)' }]}>
  <h1>SENTINEL  -  Adversarial Reviewer</h1>

  <p>
    SENTINEL is the <strong>reviewer</strong>. It adversarially reviews plans and code for security, quality, and test
    coverage, and it approves the gates that let work move forward. SENTINEL reviews from its own isolated workspace
    and can request evidence from the builder — but it never reaches into the builder's filesystem.
  </p>

  <p>
    SENTINEL reviews in a fresh workspace for each evaluation, which eliminates accumulated bias. It reviews against
    the agreed plan, records a verdict, and, if it cannot verify the work, it holds the ticket rather than guessing.
    This is the "when in doubt, don't approve" principle in action: if the required evidence is missing or
    unreadable, SENTINEL does not approve.
  </p>

  <h2>What SENTINEL does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reviews the plan</strong> before any code is written, and agrees or requests changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Adversarially reviews the pull request</strong> for security, quality, and test coverage against the plan.</li>
    <li><strong>Approves gates.</strong> An approved plan unblocks building; an approved review unblocks merging.</li>
    <li><strong>Requests evidence.</strong> SENTINEL asks FORGE to run a safe, allowlisted command and reads the returned evidence — it never gets shell access to FORGE's tree.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why the review is trustworthy</h2>
  <p>
    SENTINEL's review is meaningful because it's isolated:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>SENTINEL <strong>never touches FORGE's filesystem</strong>. Builder and reviewer don't share a tree.</li>
    <li>SENTINEL only interacts with FORGE's code by requesting a safe command and reading the evidence.</li>
    <li>A verification result that wasn't <strong>durably recorded</strong> cannot approve a gate.</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout type="warning" title="Strict by design">
    SENTINEL would rather block than merge something unverified. If the evidence isn't there, it holds the ticket
    or escalates — it never approves on a guess. This single strictness is worth more than any individual control.
  </Callout>
</DocsLayout>
